infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title When XFetch wins and when it loses
  desc Read rate per key decides it — XFetch needs many reads in the final seconds before TTL
  compares
    - label Hot key — use XFetch
      children
        - label Frequent reads
          desc Read every ~100 ms over a 60 s TTL — many chances to fire the threshold
        - label ≥ 10 reads per rebuild window
          desc The rule of thumb: enough reads in the last delta seconds before TTL
        - label Zero coordination cost
          desc One rand() per read, no Redis round-trip, no lock
        - label ~1 early rebuild per TTL window
          desc Holds at any fleet size — 200 nodes, 50,000 reads/s still fire ~1
    - label Cold key — use lock or SWR
      children
        - label Sporadic reads
          desc Read once per 30 s with TTL=60 s — one or two reads in the whole window
        - label Window may pass with zero reads
          desc Narrow ~1 s early-refresh window is easy to miss entirely
        - label Misses then stampedes
          desc Key expires normally and every reader rebuilds at once
        - label Fall back coordination-first
          desc A distributed lock or stale-while-revalidate covers cold keys
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #f43f5e
